Here's a function in JavaScript that takes an array of 5 numbers as input and checks if any of the numbers is a valid prime number. If it is, the number is printed to the console:

```javascript
function checkPrime(arr) {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    let num = arr[i];
    if (num === 0 || num === 1) {
      console.log("0 and 1 are not prime numbers.");
      break;
    }
    for (let j = 2; j < num; j++) {
      if (num % j == 0) {
        console.log(`${num} is not a prime number.`);
        break;
      }
    }
    if (j == num) {
      console.log(`${num} is a prime number.`);
    }
  }
}

let inputArr = [5, 7, 13, 17, 33];
checkPrime(inputArr);
```
In this function, we first check if the number is either 0 or 1, because those aren't considered prime numbers. Then, we loop through the array and check if the number is evenly divisible by any number between 2 and the number itself (excluding the number itself). If it is, the number is not prime. We also use a variable called "j" to keep track of the current number we are checking, and we increment it by 1 each time we exit the loop.

If the number doesn't have any factors in this range (i.e. it is prime), we print the message "is a prime number". If the loop completes and the number is still not divisible by any number in the range, it is a prime number and we print the message "is a prime number."

L-theanine is an amino acid that is found primarily in tea and foods such as mushrooms. Numerous in vitro (lab culture) and animal studies on humans suggest l-theanine can affect the following chemicals in the brain to produce a calming effect:

  Serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine, which are neurotransmitters that play a role in maintaining mood, memory, and concentration;
  Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), which is an inhibitory neurotransmitter that normally lowers the excitability of nerve cells in the central nervous system. A lack of GABA in the body can contribute to anxiety disorders;
  Glutamate, which is thought to play a role in the formation of synapses (brain connections) and in regulating stress responses; and
  Brain proteins involved in emotional regulation such as N-acetylaspartate.

Keep in mind that there is currently not enough evidence to prove that l-theanine helps with anxiety disorders or sleep disturbance in humans. However, most studies show it is generally considered safe with few reported side effects.

